 "lead": "Base language models acquire the referent of *you* in five ordered stages. Every base of 0.6B parameters or more trained on trillions of tokens reaches the first three. The fifth stage is tracking *you* after objects change hands. **No public model up to 8B reaches it zero-shot** (with no worked examples), at any token count measured up to 36T, and every one of them has a negative swap shift. From 14B to 1T parameters, stage 5 is weak and nearly flat. In these models, parameter count and depth grow together. **kotodama-tsumugi** separates the two. It is a 1.4B model run as 72 effective layers (looped depth, boundary-fitted attention residuals). It reaches stage 3 by **33B tokens** (annealed; on the constant-LR trunk by 40B). That is the token cost of OLMo-2 13B, against about 420B for OLMo-2 1B. At 110B tokens its zero-shot stage-5 reading (−.014) is not inverted under the gauge rule. Every 1–2B public base whose static *you* binds inverts, except Llama-3.2-1B. In a check of 20 bases, kotodama-tsumugi (.335) follows a hand-off told in the third person better than every Pythia model up to 6.9B (.275 to .299) and Llama-3.2-1B (.304). Most bases up to 1.5B barely follow one: 7 of those 10 score .26 to .34, against about .29 by chance. For every model of this size, that hand-off is the open step toward stage 5.",
